{"input_text": "Question: Here I have a question to to calculate  $$\int_{0}^{2}e^{x}dx$$  using Riemann sums. I got to the part where  $$\int_{0}^{2}e^{x}dx=\lim....(e^{\frac{2i}{n}})\frac{2}{n}=\lim....(e^{\frac{2(\frac{r-r^{n+1}}{1-r})}{n}})\frac{2}{n}$$ 
What should I do now? Can I expand this equation further? It doesnt seem like it. Thank you for helping. 

Answer: \begin{align}
\int_0^2e^xdx&=\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\frac2{n}e^{2i/n}\\
&=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac2{n}\left(\frac{e^{2}-1}{e^{2/n}-1}\right)\\
&=e^2-1.
\end{align} 
